<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.3.2: Writing Statements from Function Notation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00882</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.3.2: Writing Statements from Function Notation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>96c04e28-5f09-4dae-86af-ad0317a73d06</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="two cell phones">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_06_03_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <para>The function \(P\) gives the number of people, in millions, who own a smartphone, \(t\) years after the year 2000. So, any value for which \(t\) is negative would represent the number of years before 2000.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does this equation tell us about smartphone ownership?
        <para>\(P(17)=2320\)</para>
      </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>In 2017, about 2,320 million or 2.32 billion people owned a smartphone. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does this equation tell us about smartphone ownership?</para>
          <para>\(P(-10)=0\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No one owned a smartphone in 1990, 10 years before 2000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Use function notation to represent this statement.
        <para>In 2010, the number of people who owned a smartphone was 296,600,000.</para>
      </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(P(10) = 296.6\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Use function notation to represent this statement.
          <para>In 2015, about 1.86 billion people owned a smartphone.</para>
        </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(P(15) = 1860\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Mai is curious about the value of \(t\) in \(P(t)=1000\).
        <para>What would the value of \(t\) tell Mai about the situation?</para>
      </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> The year when 1 billion people owned a smartphone (adding the value of \(t\) to 2000 gives the year)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Mai is curious about the value of \(t\) in \(P(t)=1000\).</para>
          <para>Is 4 a possible value of \(t\) here?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No. \(t=4\) corresponds to the year 2004. At that time, the number of smartphone owners should be below that of 2010, which was 296 million.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
        <item>Use your own graph paper to sketch a graph of the function. 
    
    Your graph should go through these four points: \((-10,0)\), \((10,296.6)\), \((15,1860)\), and \((17,2320)\).
  </item>
      </list>
      <div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Nonlinear function. years since 2000 and population in millions.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_06_03_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <title>Video: Understanding Equations in Function Notation </title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about reading equations in function notation. </para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c20560ec6ea05974bbc3479ca6a0ddadd1bbbffa"/>
      </media>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking </title>
          <para>What can you say about the value or values of \(t\) when \(P(t)=1000\)?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer: </para>
          <para>There is not enough information to know exactly when 1 billion (1,000 million) people owned a smartphone, but there is likely only one value of \(t\) when \(P(t)=1000\), and that value is somewhere between 10 and 15. This is because the number of smartphone users was less than a billion in 2010 and more than a billion in 2015. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
  </content>
